From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kern0x1b <kern0x1b@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 03:13:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] Take the job count from a jobserver or from
 XMAKE_MAX_JOBS

xmake schedules its own jobs with os.default_njob(), the number of
cores plus two, and passes that number as -j to every package build it
runs, so a machine shared by several builds runs each of them at full
width: a package build that is told to keep to six jobs runs fourteen,
and an LLVM build in a package install reached a load of 66 on twelve
cores.

os.default_njob() now returns XMAKE_MAX_JOBS when it is set, and xmake
becomes a client of a GNU make jobserver when MAKEFLAGS names one with
--jobserver-auth=fifo:PATH, the form GNU make 4.4 and ninja 1.13 read:

- core.base.jobserver opens the FIFO once per process and takes and
  gives back single tokens without blocking (io.jobserver_open, _take,
  _give and _close, in C, because a buffered file read would take
  every token there is at once). hold(n, func) takes up to n - 1 tokens
  that are free, calls func with the width it got, and gives them back.
- runjobs runs the first job of each call on the implicit token of the
  process and takes a token from the jobserver for every other job it
  runs at the same time, and gives it back when the job ends. A call
  that waits for a token sleeps in its coroutine, so the jobs it already
  runs finish and give theirs back.
- ninja and meson leave -j out when the jobserver is there and ninja is
  1.13 or later, so ninja takes its tokens from the jobserver as it
  goes; an explicit jobs option of a recipe still wins.
- make, autoconf and CMake's Makefile builds run make with -j set to
  the tokens they hold, because the make of macOS is 3.81 and reads no
  FIFO.
